    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d541">

                <pb facs="0969" n="865" sameAs="frus:frus1943CairoTehran/pg865"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to President
                        Roosevelt</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d541-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-01-07">7 January 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>No. 536. Prime Minister to President Roosevelt. Personal and most secret.</p>
                <p>Bedell Smith and Devers came through here morning of 5th. Bedell told me that he
                    and Montgomery are convinced that it is better to put in a much heavier and
                    broader <hi rendition="#small-caps">Overlord</hi> than to expand <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">Anvil</hi> above our pre-Teheran conception and that
                    he is putting this to Eisenhower and your Chiefs of Staff.…</p>
                <p>It also seems to me from what I heard very probable that the Y Moon (see my
                    immediately following)<anchor n="2" xml:id="d541-f2"/> will be at the earliest
                    practicable date. I do not see why we should resist this if the Commanders feel
                    they have a better chance then. At Teheran, however, C. O. S. recommendation was
                    Y1 or one day earlier which you and I agreed to express more agreeably as
                    “During May”.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d541-f3"/> In conversation with U. J. we
                    never mentioned such a date as May 5th or May 8th but always spoke to him around
                        20th.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d541-f4"/> Neither did we at any time dwell upon
                    the exact phase of the operation which should fall on any particular <pb
                        facs="0970" n="866" xml:id="pg866"/> day. If now the Y date is accepted as
                    final I do not feel that we shall in any way have broken faith with him. The
                    operation will anyhow begin in May with feints and softening bombardments and I
                    do not think IT. J. is the kind of man to be unreasonable over 48 hours.<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d541-f5"/></p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d541-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d541-f1-content">
                <p>Sent by the United States Military Attaché, London, via Army channels.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d541-f2" n="2" xml:id="d541-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 537 reads: “… Y date is June 2d.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d541-f3" n="3" xml:id="d541-f3-content">
                <p>See <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg564">p. 564</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d541-f4" n="4" xml:id="d541-f4-content">
                <p>See <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg547">p. 547</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d541-f5" n="5" xml:id="d541-f5-content">
                <p>Further references of 1944 by Roosevelt, Churchill, and Stalin to the Tehran
                    agreement on the invasion of Europe will be found in <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stalin’s Correspondence</hi>, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp.
                    138, 145.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
